# English versions of the blog posts in yazilar.py (/en/articles/<slug>/).
# Keys are the Turkish slugs; sources and plant lists come from yazilar.py. Plant links are written {id}.

Y_EN = {
'kuzey-peruda-sifa-mesa-san-pedro-pazar': {'slug': 'healing-in-northern-peru-mesa-san-pedro-market',
 'baslik': 'Healing in northern Peru: the mesa altar, San Pedro and the 510 plants of the market',
 'aciklama': 'From the markets of Trujillo and Chiclayo to the healers’ altars: plant healing in northern Peru today, its thousand-year roots, “seguro” bottles, flower baths and the San Pedro cactus. With sources.',
 'giris': 'Northern Peru lies at the upper end of the old Andean “health axis” that runs from southern Ecuador to northern Bolivia. The ethnobotanist Rainer Bussmann and the anthropologist Douglas Sharon worked for '
          'more than a decade in the region’s markets and alongside its healers. This article summarises the region’s healing tradition from their open-access study.',
 'bolumler': [('The plants of the market',
               ['The study began in 2001 in the big markets of Trujillo and Chiclayo. In northern Peru 510 plants with medicinal properties were recorded, with 2,499 different uses; 83 per cent of the species were '
                'native to Peru. Some 150–200 species are commonly sold in local markets; healers collect the rare ones themselves or order them from specialised collectors [1].',
                'Vendors group plants into four kinds: common and exotic plants, plants for ordinary illnesses, plants used only by healers, and plants for magical purposes. About 974 mixtures with up to 29 ingredients '
                'treat 164 conditions; around 65 per cent of the plants are used in mixtures [1].',
                'The tradition is not frozen. Compared with the plants in the collection Bishop Martínez Compañón sent to Spain in 1789, half of the plants used in colonial times have disappeared from the list, yet the '
                'total has grown. The recent spread of noni products in the markets shows that herbalists watch international health trends closely [1].']),
              ('Most plants go to “magical” illnesses',
               ['The largest number of species, 207, is used for “magical” ailments, more than a quarter of all uses. Bad air (mal aire), bad wind, fright (susto or espanto), the evil eye (mal ojo) and sorcery (daño, '
                'brujería) are considered very common illnesses in Andean society. According to the researchers, the Western idea of “psychosomatic disorders” comes closest to describing them [1].',
                'Treatment often involves a cleansing ceremony, a limpia: this can be a simple spraying with holy water and perfume, or an all-night ceremony at the healer’s altar. In the days after the ceremony the '
                'patient takes a “flowering bath” (baño de florecimiento). The patient also receives a “seguro”: a bottle filled with powerful herbs, perfumes, images of saints and the patient’s hair and nail '
                'clippings, carried for protection and luck [1].']),
              ('Mesa: at the altar',
               ['Healers usually treat patients at the mesa altar set up in their back yard, sometimes at sacred places in the countryside or on the shores of sacred lagoons high in the mountains. On the altar, “power '
                'objects” such as seashells, pre-Columbian ceramics, staffs and stones stand side by side with crosses and images of saints [1].',
                'The ceremony begins with the healer spraying blessed plant extracts from his mouth over the patient’s whole body. In most ceremonies the patient drinks boiled {sanpedro} juice and takes tobacco '
                '({mapacho}) juice and perfume through the nose; sometimes {toe} (Brugmansia) is used too. In the chants called tarjos the healers invoke the mountain spirits (apus) and sacred lagoons as well as '
                'Christ, the Virgin and the saints. The guinea pig (cuy) is a standard tool of diagnosis [1].']),
              ('A three-thousand-year-old cactus',
               ['The use of the San Pedro cactus goes back thousands of years. Its remains have been found at Guitarrero Cave in the Andes (8200–6800 BCE), at Las Aldas on the north-central coast (2000–1500 BCE) and at '
                'Garagay on the central coast. The cactus is often depicted in Cupisnique, Chavín, Moche, Nazca and Lambayeque art [1].',
                'Among the plants whose use goes back to the Moche period (100–800 CE), besides San Pedro, are maichil ({camalonga}) and ishpingo: they are depicted on Moche ceramics, and remains of some were found in '
                'elite burials such as the tomb of the Lord of Sipán [1][2]. Despite five hundred years of suppression the tradition survived; in 2022 Peru’s Ministry of Culture declared the traditional knowledge of '
                'San Pedro part of the nation’s cultural heritage [3].']),
              ('Threats',
               ['According to Bussmann and Sharon, the greatest threat to this ancient tradition is the loss of plant habitats: urban sprawl and sugar, cotton and rice agribusiness have changed the coastal plains; '
                'climate change and deforestation threaten the mountain forests. The high Andean ecosystems and sacred lagoons where many medicinal plants grow are endangered by large-scale mining [1].',
                'Demand is growing but cultivation is not: most plants are still gathered from the wild, and some important species show signs of overharvesting. The researchers also tested the plants in the '
                'laboratory: antibacterial activity was confirmed in most plants used for infections, but 24 per cent of water extracts and 76 per cent of alcohol extracts were toxic. Traditional preparation methods '
                'take this into account when choosing the solvent [1]. San Pedro and Brugmansia are powerful and dangerous plants; they are described here as a cultural record.'])]},
'dunya-agaclari-kayin-yggdrasil-ceiba-bodhi': {'slug': 'world-trees-birch-yggdrasil-ceiba-bodhi',
 'baslik': 'World trees: the birch, Yggdrasil, the ceiba and the Bodhi tree',
 'aciklama': 'The birch reaching to the sky in the Altai, the ash that holds up the world in the Edda, the Maya world axis ceiba, the date palm of Assyrian palaces and the fig under which the Buddha '
             'awoke: the tree at the centre of the world, with sources.',
 'giris': 'Cultures thousands of kilometres apart have placed a tree at the centre of the universe. With its roots in the underworld, its trunk on earth and its branches in the sky, this tree joins '
          'the three worlds. This article sets side by side the world trees we describe separately on the site.',
 'bolumler': [('The birch in the Altai',
               ['Turkic mythology has a “cosmic tree” at the centre of the universe reaching to the Pole Star, named in the sources Bay Kayın or Altınkazık [1]. According to Altai tales a birch '
                'rises on the summit of the world mountain. Kams do not perform rites without a birch; in the Altai horse sacrifice they pass through the layers of the sky by climbing a nine-stepped '
                'birch set up in the middle of the tent [1]. In Central Asian Turkic “kayın” means birch.']),
              ('The ash in the Edda: Yggdrasil',
               ['In the Norse Eddic poems the tree that holds up the world is Yggdrasil, an ever-green ash standing at the well of Urd. Its three roots reach over the land of the dead, the frost '
                'giants and humankind. An eagle lives at its top and the dragon Nidhogg at its foot; the squirrel Ratatosk carries words between them [2]. The likeness to the bird at the top and the '
                'serpent at the foot of the Turkic tree of life is striking.']),
              ('The Maya ceiba',
               ['In Classic Maya cosmology the giant ceiba (Ceiba pentandra) is the axis of the world; its roots reach into the underworld and it is said to hold up the world. In Maya, Aztec and '
                'Mixtec art the conical spines of the young ceiba trunk were modelled in clay on incense burners and funerary urns. The ceiba is today the national tree of Guatemala [3].',
                'The same tree is sacred in the Amazon too: healers around Iquitos in Peru count the ceiba, which they call “lupuna”, among the plants that teach medicine when taken with a diet '
                '[4].']),
              ('The date palm in Assyria',
               ['In the palace of the Assyrian king Ashurnasirpal II at Nimrud (9th century BC), stylised trees stand beneath the winged sun-disc, flanked by guardian figures. Norma Franklin '
                'identifies them as female date palms with their offshoots and reads them as a symbol of the king’s lineage [5].']),
              ('The Buddha’s tree: Bodhi',
               ['Siddhartha gained enlightenment under a pipal tree (Ficus religiosa) at Gaya, so the tree became the Bodhi, or wisdom, tree. In early Buddhist reliefs the Buddha is not shown in '
                'human form; he is represented by the Bodhi tree and the empty throne beneath it [6].']),
              ('What do they share?',
               ['All these trees speak of a centre, an axis and a connection: between underworld and sky, between the dead and the living, between people and gods. Tying cloth to a tree in Anatolia '
                'or making a wish at the foot of an old plane tree is one of the forms in which this very old idea survives. Each community’s story is its own; what is reported here is what the '
                'sources say.'])]},
'anadoluda-bitkiyle-tedavinin-tarihi-hititlerden-osmanliya': dict(
    slug='history-of-plant-medicine-in-anatolia',
    baslik='A short history of plant medicine in Anatolia: from the Hittites to the Ottomans',
    aciklama='From the flower burial at Shanidar to Hittite tablets, Seljuk hospitals and the first Ottoman drug books in Turkish: the long journey of healing-plant knowledge in Anatolia.',
    giris='Anyone who buys linden or sage from a herbalist in Turkey today stands at the end of a chain of knowledge that goes back thousands of years in Anatolia. The history chapter of Turhan Baytop’s Türkiye’de Bitkiler ile Tedavi (Plant Healing in Turkey) follows that chain from Neanderthal graves to Ottoman hospitals. This article is a short summary of that chapter.',
    bolumler=[
        ('Prehistory: the flowers of Shanidar', [
            'According to Baytop, people in Anatolia have used plants for healing since the Palaeolithic. As the earliest evidence he points to the plant remains found in Neanderthal graves in Shanidar Cave, just south of Hakkari [1]. Whether those remains were left in a ritual or carried into the cave by animals is still debated.',
        ]),
        ('Mesopotamia: 250 plant drugs', [
            'Sumerian, Akkadian and Assyrian tablets show that healing was carried out by priest-physicians using both magic and medicine. Most remedies were made from plants; beer, wine and butter served as carriers. Care was taken to gather plants at particular times, at night or before sunrise or after sunset, and to dry them in the shade [1].',
            'About 250 plant drugs are known from this period. Plants we still recognise today, such as {adamotu}, henbane, {hashas}, {safran}, thyme, mint and fennel, appear often in the prescriptions [1].',
        ]),
        ('The Hittites: “And so the patient will be well”', [
            'We know Hittite medicine from the tablets found in the archive of their capital Hattusa (Boğazköy). The Hittites saw illness as a punishment from the gods and used magic and medicine together. Most of their remedies were plant-based; the Hittite word for a plant drug was “waššī” [1].',
            'Alongside Anatolian plants such as {alic}, almond, {defne}, {meyan}, {mersin}, {sarimsak} and olive, the prescriptions also mention imported drugs such as myrrh and asafoetida. The Hittites cultivated some plants and sold others, such as saffron and opium poppy, abroad [1].',
            'Amounts are rarely given; the tablets say only “a little” or “much”. Whether the remedy is to be taken by night or by day, however, is stated. Every prescription ends with the sentence “And so the patient will be well”; the next one begins “If he does not get well” [1].',
        ]),
        ('The Seljuks: a hospital in every city', [
            'In the two centuries from 1071 to 1308, the Anatolian Seljuks founded hospitals called bimaristan, darülafiye or darüşşifa in almost every large city. As Baytop reports, these were fully equipped institutions with their own physicians and pharmacists [1].',
            'According to Seljuk endowment deeds, shops selling medicines in the cities stocked plants brought even from India, and caravanserai hospitals had pharmacies of their own. The first of these hospitals is the Gevher Nesibe Sultan Hospital, founded in Kayseri in 1206. The two snake reliefs on its gate can still be seen; the building is now a museum [1].',
        ]),
        ('The Ottomans: herb gatherer, syrup maker and pharmacist', [
            'Ottoman medicine largely continued Seljuk and Islamic medicine; the main difference is that it was written in Turkish rather than Arabic or Persian. The endowment deed of the hospital that Sultan Bayezid I built in Bursa in 1399 names three people who worked with medicines: the şerbetçi, who made syrups and sherbets; the aşşab, who knew the medicinal plants and gathered or bought them; and the saydelan, the pharmacist who prepared pastes, pills and the main medicines [1].',
            'The oldest known drug book written in Turkish is the Edviye-i Müfrede of İshak bin Murad of Gerede, dated 1389–90. In plain Turkish it lists remedies in alphabetical order, with where each is found, what it is good for and what harm it can do. Baytop notes that most of the plants in this book are still known among the people by the same names and used for the same purposes [1].',
            'In the fifteenth century Sabuncuoğlu Şerefeddin, chief physician of the Amasya hospital, ran an experiment to see whether his antidotes worked against snake venom: he had a viper bite a rooster, then gave it the antidote and recorded the result [1]. In the same century the physician Şirvânî, who came to Anatolia from Shirvan, was writing about how to detect counterfeit drugs, including how he told real {zerdecal} from fake.',
        ]),
        ('A note', [
            'This article is a short summary of the history chapter of Baytop’s book; see the book for details and primary sources. It is not always certain which modern species the plant names in old prescriptions refer to.',
        ]),
    ]),
'ogreten-bitkiler-dieta-ayahuasca-pastora': dict(
    slug='teacher-plants-dieta-ayahuasca-pastora',
    baslik='Teacher plants: the dieta in the Amazon and the leaf of the Pastora in the Mazatec mountains',
    aciklama='The dieta healers undertake with “plants that have a mother” in the Peruvian Amazon, the mother of mapacho tobacco who appears in dreams, the language of ritual in ayahuasca tourism, and Salvia divinorum in Mexico: with sources.',
    giris='In some communities a plant is not only a medicine but a teacher. In the Peruvian Amazon, healers believe that certain plants have a “mother” who comes in dreams and visions to teach. In the Mazatec mountains of Mexico, a sacred leaf bears a name that evokes the Virgin Mary. This article draws on field studies from both worlds.',
    bolumler=[
        ('Plants that have a mother', [
            'In a field study carried out between 2003 and 2008 in the east-central Peruvian Amazon, researchers interviewed 29 healers, 3 apprentices and 4 herbalists; some were Shipibo-Konibo, Asháninka and Matsigenka, others from mestizo communities. They recorded 55 plant species from 26 families used in preparing to become a healer [1].',
            'Locally these plants are called “plantas con madre”, plants with a mother. Each is believed to have a spirit, a mother; when the apprentice takes the plant, the mother teaches the uses of plants, the path of healing and sacred knowledge [1].',
        ]),
        ('The dieta: alone in the forest, without salt, in silence', [
            'The path to this learning runs through a regimen called the “dieta”. It has four core elements: withdrawing into the forest and speaking to no one except the master healer; taking one or more plants every day; giving up salt, sugar, fat, pork, game, sour foods, garlic, hot sauce, alcohol and cold drinks, sometimes fasting completely; and sexual abstinence [1].',
            'The master healer gives the plants in a set order. First come plants for purging and cleansing, then plants that open sensitivity and intuition, then strengthening plants, and last the plants that protect and defend [1]. Among other Amazonian peoples too, healers in training seek to reach the spirit world through seclusion, fasting and abstinence [1].',
        ]),
        ('The mother of tobacco', [
            'The strong tobacco called “rome” in Shipibo-Konibo and known locally as mapacho (Nicotiana rustica) is considered one of the most powerful and oldest plants. According to one healer, mapacho balances negative energies in the body and teaches a person to become a warrior who cleanses himself; its mother appears in dreams as a large, short-haired, dark-skinned man in silver-embroidered clothing [1].',
            'Two healers described the mother of tobacco as twins: these twins teach the icaros, the healing songs, and how to blow smoke over the patient [1].',
        ]),
        ('Ayahuasquero or curandero?', [
            'Today people from all over the world travel to the Amazon, especially around Iquitos in Peru, to take part in ayahuasca ceremonies. An ethnographic study in this area shows that local people draw a distinction: someone who leads ayahuasca ceremonies is an “ayahuasquero”, while a “curandero” is a healer who can treat many ailments with many plants. The locals interviewed stressed that not everyone who leads ceremonies is a curandero [2].',
            'The same study examines how the long talks that shamans give before and during ceremonies for tourists frame the experience. These talks set the ceremonial space apart from everyday life and shape how participants make sense of what they go through [2]. The ceremonies also include icaros sung partly in Quechua and other Indigenous languages [2].',
            'We tell the chemistry of the plant pair that makes ayahuasca possible, and why it interacts dangerously with medicines, in a separate story: the drink works because substances in the vine stop the DMT in the chacruna leaf from being broken down in digestion [4].',
        ]),
        ('The leaf of the Pastora in the Mazatec mountains', [
            'Salvia divinorum is a large, velvety-leaved sage that grows wild only in the Mazatec mountains of southern Mexico. The Mazatec call it “xka pastora” in their language, a name that links the plant to the Virgin Mary [3].',
            'Outside the region the plant is known almost only for its psychoactive effect, but locally its use is much broader. People interviewed apply the leaves externally for joint pain, swelling and inflammation, insect stings, sore throat and headache, and drink a mild tea of a few fresh leaves for headache, fever and general pain. Some also use it to treat animals [3].',
            'The Mazatec grow the plant near their homes but keep it out of sight, because it is a sacred being whose rules must be followed. The researcher noticed that the first plant she saw was bent like an arch: in these mountains an arch shows that something is sacred. Arches are also set up around Day of the Dead altars and at the entrances of houses holding weddings and baptisms [3].',
            'The study calls the way drug research reduces the plant to its active compound “stripping”, and the way the Mazatec wrap it in prayers, rules and relationships “dressing”; it stresses that this difference has ethical and political consequences for Indigenous peoples connected to the plant [3].',
        ]),
        ('A note', [
            'This article reports what field studies describe; it is not an account of personal experience or a recommendation for use. Ayahuasca and Salvia divinorum are plants with powerful effects, are regulated in many countries and can interact seriously with medicines.',
        ]),
    ]),
'kutsal-agaclar-kayin-umay-hayat-agaci': dict(
    slug='sacred-trees-birch-umay-tree-of-life',
    baslik='Sacred trees: the birch, Umay and the tree of life',
    aciklama='The birch reaching to the sky among the early Turks, the two trees that came down to earth with Umay, the birch root in the cradle and the custom of tying rags to trees that reached Anatolia: with sources.',
    giris='Someone in Anatolia who ties a strip of cloth to a tree, makes a wish at its foot or cannot bear to see an old plane tree cut down may be following the trace of a very old belief. For the early Turks a tree was not just wood or shade; it was an axis joining earth and sky, the place where a lineage began and the ladder by which the shaman climbed to heaven.',
    bolumler=[
        ('The tree that joins earth and sky', [
            'Turkic mythology has the idea of a “cosmic tree” standing at the centre of the universe and reaching to the Pole Star. In the sources it appears under names such as Demirkazık, Altınkazık, Bay Kayın or the Tree of Saints [1]. Its roots reach into the underworld, its trunk stands on earth and its branches rise into the sky, linking the three worlds [2].',
            'Other cultures have a similar tree. In Norse mythology the tree that holds up the universe is Yggdrasil. In Mesopotamia the tree of life became a symbol of abundance and fertility, and among the Assyrians a symbol of empire [2]. In Turkic narratives a bird, sometimes a double-headed eagle, often perches at the very top of the tree of life [1].',
        ]),
        ('Why the birch?', [
            'The sacred trees most often named in old Turkic narratives are the white pine, larch, birch, cedar, plane, linden and cypress, but the birch comes first [1][2]. According to Altai tales a birch rises on the summit of the world mountain, and in the sacred hollow beneath it lies the water of life [1].',
            'The kams (shamans) of Turkic peoples such as the Sagay, Altai, Shor and Teleut are known not to perform a rite without a birch beside them. Shaman drums almost always show a birch alongside the sun, the moon and lightning. During the rite the branches of the birch stand for the ladder the shaman uses to climb to the sky [1][3]. When a kam died, the drum was broken and hung from the branch of a birch [1].',
            'Kam prayers address the birch directly, calling it the golden-leaved, eight-shadowed, nine-rooted “bay kayın” (rich birch) [1]. The Sagay and Beltir make offerings to the sky or the mountain beneath birch trees; the Sakha hold the larch sacred together with the birch [1].',
            'A small botanical note: in the Turkic languages of Central Asia “kayın” means birch (Betula). In Anatolia the same word is given to a different tree, the beech (Fagus) of Turkish forests. So the white-trunked “kayın” of the myths is in fact the white-barked birch.',
        ]),
        ('The two trees that came down with Umay', [
            'A Sagay kam explained the two birches painted on his drum this way: when we descended from our ancestor Ülgen, these two birch trees came down from the sky to earth together with Mother Umay [1][3]. The sanctity of the birch is therefore tied to creation and to the beginning of the lineage.',
            'In early Turkic belief Umay is the mother figure who helps at birth, protects mother and baby and grants kut, divine favour. In her depictions the birch is among the most frequent symbols, together with hair, comb, thread, bird, bow and arrow; she is also associated with the mountain, the cave, fire and the hearth [3].',
        ]),
        ('The birch root in the cradle', [
            'In the Altai a small birch root was placed in a newborn’s cradle, and the cradle was hung from birch branches; the root of the birch was believed to nourish the baby. If a baby died, it was wrapped in birch bark and laid in a hollow of the tree. The Khakas also laid babies in the hollow of a sacred birch, believing that their souls would go not to the underworld but to the White Milk Lake in the sky [3].',
            'The birch is present as birth approaches too: the shaman stretches horsehair cords in several directions across the tent where the woman lies, hung with figures of the sun, moon and birds cut from birch bark. Among the Teleut, before a child is laid in the cradle for the first time, the oldest woman purifies it with {ardic} smoke [3]. Belief in the cleansing power of juniper smoke is still alive in Anatolia today.',
        ]),
        ('Traces that survive in Anatolia', [
            'While the white pine, larch and birch were held sacred in Turkestan, in Anatolia this reverence passed to the pine, poplar, juniper, cedar, cypress, oak, mulberry, willow, apple and above all the plane tree [1]. Tying coloured rags to trees is one of the forms in which this old tree belief survives today [1].',
            'After Islam the date palm, apple, pomegranate and rose came to the fore. The decorated “nahıl” carried in Ottoman wedding and circumcision processions means date palm [1]. In folk tales one of the most common motifs is a childless couple sharing an apple; the apple stands for wishes fulfilled, children and beauty. The mulberry planted in gardens and cemeteries is part of everyday life through its shade, its fruit and the wood it gives for the saz [4].',
        ]),
        ('Wishing for a child, and the tree', [
            'The link between trees and fertility also appears in the birth rituals of the Shamanist Turks. It is reported that among the Kazakhs childless women rolled under an apple tree in order to conceive, and that among the Kyrgyz people visited sacred trees when wishing for a child [5]. The wheat and sugar thrown over a bride’s head at Anatolian weddings today is seen as a continuation of the custom, among farming Turkic communities, of throwing wheat, millet and fruit over the bride [5].',
        ]),
        ('A note', [
            'The beliefs in this article come from narratives of Turkic peoples recorded at different times and from academic studies of those narratives. Not every community believes the same things; what is reported here is what the sources say.',
        ]),
    ]),
'samanizm-ve-kam-gelenegi': dict(
    slug='what-is-shamanism-kam-tradition',
    baslik='What is shamanism? The kam tradition from Siberia to Anatolia',
    aciklama='The origin of the word shaman, the kam tradition of the Turks, the drum and the sacred tree, juniper smoke and the traces that survive in Anatolia: with sources.',
    giris='The word “shaman” is used very broadly today, for everyone from healers in the Amazon to weekend workshops. Yet the word has an origin and the concept a particular geography. In this article we look at what shamanism is, at the “kam” tradition of the Turks and at the ties this tradition has with plants.',
    bolumler=[
        ('Origin and meaning of the word', [
            'Shamanism is a religious phenomenon centred on a person, the shaman, who is believed to gain power through trance or ecstasy. The shaman is believed to heal illness, to communicate with the spirit world and often to guide the souls of the dead to that world [1].',
            'The word comes from the Tungusic languages of Siberia. A link with the Sanskrit “śramaṇa” (ascetic) has also been proposed, though some researchers reject it [2].',
        ]),
        ('The kam among the Turks', [
            'In Altai and Tatar the shaman is called “kam”. The word appears in Old Uyghur texts, in the Kutadgu Bilig and in the Codex Cumanicus [2]. One of the most comprehensive Turkish-language studies of shamanism in the Turkic world is Abdülkadir İnan’s Tarihte ve Bugün Şamanizm (Shamanism in History and Today), published in 1954 [3].',
            'In the shamanist world view the universe is usually divided into three layers: sky, earth and underworld. Among the Altai the benevolent sky being is Ülgen, and the evil ruler of the underworld is Erlik Khan [2].',
        ]),
        ('Drum and tree: the road to the sky', [
            'The kam’s most important instrument is the drum. Among the Altai the drum, called “tüngür” or “çaluu”, is made after the call to become a kam has been received; its frame is of birch or cedar chosen from untouched forest. The sun, the moon, animals and trees are painted on its face; Altai drums also show the horse that carries the kam to the sky [2].',
            'In this world the tree is not merely a material but a road. In the Altai horse sacrifice recorded by the linguist Wilhelm Radloff in 1884 and retold by Mircea Eliade, a young birch with nine steps cut into it is set up in the middle of a newly pitched tent. Climbing the steps one by one, the kam passes through the layers of the sky and reaches the realm of Bay Ülgen [4].',
            'Among the early Turks some trees such as the birch were held sacred; shamanists believed that their ancestors came from trees and that their souls lived in trees [5].',
        ]),
        ('Smoke and plants', [
            'In shamanic ceremonies plants are often bound up with smoke. In Kyrgyz, Kazakh and Mongolian shamanism {ardic} (archa) smoke drives away evil spirits; before ceremonies the place and the people are purified with archa. Mongolian and Siberian peoples also use {pelin} (Artemisia) smoke for purification.',
            'In north-eastern Siberia, Koryak, Chukchi and Evenk shamans are recorded as using dried {sinekmantari} to enter trance and journey to the spirit world. This mushroom is poisonous; it appears here only as a cultural record.',
        ]),
        ('Traces that survive in Anatolia', [
            'Many Turkic communities lived with shamanism before adopting Buddhism, Manichaeism or Islam. The language historian Zeynep Korkmaz examines how some elements of the old Turkic tree cult continued into the Islamic period [5].',
            'According to Ülkü Gürsoy, tying cloth to the branches of a mulberry tree while making a wish is a custom rooted in old Turkic belief; in the Alevi-Bektashi tradition the mulberry has kept its sanctity and is considered “the soul of the house” [6]. The French Turkologist Irène Mélikoff also drew attention to the shamanic roots of the semah of the Forty in Anatolian Alevism [2].',
            'The {uzerlik} hung at the door, the seeds burned on the hearth and the cloth tied to a tree mostly live on today as “custom”; their roots reach thousands of kilometres east, to the steppe and the taiga.',
        ]),
        ('A note', [
            'Shamanism is not a single religion but a common name for many similar local traditions. On Bitki Tabanı these traditions are described as a cultural record, with the names of the communities that keep them alive and with sources; they are not recommendations for ritual use.',
        ]),
    ]),
}
